WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:05.080
There are lots of good reasons
to try and follow a healthier diet—

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.160
you lose weight,
you feel good,

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.000
but the main reason?

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.520
To live a longer, happier,
more productive life.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.560
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.440
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.400
Today we answer what may sound
like a very unusual question:

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:28.560
can revitalizing blood with
blood transfusions slow aging?

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:29.480
In our first story,

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:31.560
we consider gruesome
experiments

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:35.320
that surgically attached old
animals to young ones

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:40.285
to show that there is something
in the blood that causes aging.

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.880
One of the major hallmarks
of aging

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:46.320
is the decline of regenerative
capacity of our tissues.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:48.960
There are stem cells residing
in our muscles, for example,

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.480
that can leap into service
at the first sign of injury

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:53.480
to repair any damage.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.240
Is the waning
of tissue renewing abilities

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:59.600
due to some intrinsic property
of aging stem cells

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:03.960
or a consequence of being
trapped in an aging body?

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:08.440
To find out, researchers grafted
the muscles of old rats

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:11.000
into young rats and vice-versa.

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:12.360
Inside the young rat,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.480
even the weak atrophied muscles

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:17.280
of extremely old rats
at the end of their lives

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.680
regained their strength, volume,
and ability to regenerate.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:22.040
They became young again,

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:25.720
so the capacity
was still there all along.

00:01:25.720 --> 00:01:30.000
And young muscles in old rats
lost renewal capacity,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.840
so it appeared to be something
about the surrounding milieu

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.320
rather than
inherent defects with age.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.840
To see if the critical elements

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.960
were circulating
in the bloodstream,

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:42.240
old muscle stem cells

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.320
were cultured
in the blood of young animals.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:47.880
This alone had
a rejuvenating effect,

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.360
suggesting that there may be
some sort of vitalizing factors

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:54.840
in youth that we lose as we age,
or inversely,

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:58.600
some repair-repressing component
that builds up.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.800
Either way,
this may be good news,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:03.720
because if we can find out
what those factors are,

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:07.960
we may be able to slow aging
or even reverse it.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:10.600
Could more than just muscle
be restored?

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.440
What about the brain
and all the other organs?

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.680
To see the extent in which
circulating factors might play

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:18.240
in affecting aging,

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:23.080
researchers turned
to a macabre procedure

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:27.000
called parabiosis, from the
Greek para, meaning "next to,"

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:28.920
and bios, for "life."

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.400
It was an attempt
to recreate the phenomenon

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:33.800
of conjoined twins in a lab

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:36.760
by sewing animals together

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.840
to study the effects
of transmissible factors.

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:43.440
Conjoined twins are often
referred to as "Siamese twins,"

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:46.920
due to the notoriety of a 19th
century pair of Siamese-American

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.040
brothers joined at the chest.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:53.320
The Blazek sisters are the
only conjoined twins on record

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:55.560
ever having given birth.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:59.240
Josepha and Rosa Blazek
were quite literally

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:00.600
joined at the hip.

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.040
When Rosa got pregnant,

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.680
both of their breasts developed
and started lactating,

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.440
supporting the theory
we now know to be true today

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:11.280
that lactation is
regulated by hormones

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.120
that circulate in the blood.

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:17.280
So what about aging factors
circulating in the blood?

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:20.640
Surgically, researchers can
connect the skin, circulation,

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.920
muscle walls, body cavities,
and shoulder blade joints

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:25.640
of two animals.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:28.840
Early attempts to graft
different species failed--

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:32.480
for example, mammals to birds
or a cat to a rat,

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.400
but in 1862,
a pioneering French scientist

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:42.760
was able to successfully pair
together two young rats.

00:03:42.760 --> 00:03:45.120
The first heterochronic union--

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.040
hetero meaning "different,"
Chronos meaning "time"--

00:03:48.040 --> 00:03:51.440
the first heterochronic union
was created in 1955

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:52.960
to answer the question:

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.400
What would happen if you bathed
the tissues of an old animal

00:03:56.400 --> 00:03:58.760
in the blood of a young one?

00:03:58.760 --> 00:04:00.360
The title of the paper was,

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:04.080
"Experimental Prolongation
of the Life Span."

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:07.800
Old rats hooked up to young rats
lived about 20 percent longer

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:10.640
than old rats
hooked up to one another.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.000
Subsequent experiments showed
old mice coupled with young

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.920
became healthier, stronger,
and smarter.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:20.240
Aged tissues in a number
of organs were rejuvenated,

00:04:20.240 --> 00:04:24.560
including the brain, heart,
pancreas, skeleton, and muscles.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.480
That doesn't necessarily mean
that aging was slowed, though.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.840
Rather than some sort of
restorative bloodborne factor,

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.000
maybe the older animals
were just taking advantage

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:37.280
of the reserve organ capacity
in the younger animals,

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.320
like having an extra set
of youthful kidneys.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:43.360
To see whether there's
some aging or anti-aging

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:45.160
transmissible element,

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:49.000
rather than sharing organs and
an entire circulatory system,

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:53.000
what about just getting
a transfusion of young blood?

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:53.800
After all,

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:57.640
as an American Aging Association
journal review concluded:

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:02.080
"The use of parabiosis in humans
is currently not performed

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:03.800
due to the surgical
complications

00:05:03.800 --> 00:05:07.880
and resulting
undesirable lifestyle."

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:09.560
Ya think?

00:05:09.560 --> 00:05:13.120
But getting a transfusion
of young blood would be easy.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:19.149
Does it work?
We'll find out next.

00:05:19.654 --> 00:05:22.160
Vampires 2.0?

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:25.080
The ethical quandaries
of young blood infusion

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.040
in the quest for eternal life.

00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:31.480
In an episode of the TV series
Silicon Valley entitled,

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:33.040
"The Blood Boy,"

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:35.240
a tech guru hires
a young assistant

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.120
from whom to transfuse
pints of blood

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.960
in an attempt to slow down
his aging.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:42.160
The restoration of youth

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:45.120
by bathing in or drinking
the blood of youth

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.000
is a centuries-old trope

00:05:47.000 --> 00:05:49.240
dating back at least
to the futile attempt

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:52.240
of ailing Pope Innocent VIII,

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:56.440
who, in the 1490s,
failed to live up to his name

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.880
by apparently drinking the blood
of three ten-year-old boys,

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:01.640
who, though a bargain--

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:03.960
"costing only
one ducat apiece"--

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.800
died in the process.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.640
Might there be something
to it, though?

00:06:09.640 --> 00:06:12.320
Though young blood did
not have a significant impact

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.520
on lifespan, otherwise,

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.960
infusing the blood
of young animals into old

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.200
did have a rejuvenating effects
on multiple organs

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:22.565
similar to
full-blown parabiosis,

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:25.560
In fact, not only does the
infusion of young mouse blood

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.920
improve age-related cognitive
dysfunction in old mice,

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:32.480
young human blood,
taken from umbilical cords,

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:33.560
worked too,

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:37.040
in mice engineered to not reject
the foreign tissue.

00:06:37.040 --> 00:06:39.080
This led
to ongoing clinical trials

00:06:39.080 --> 00:06:41.520
offering weekly infusions
of blood products

00:06:41.520 --> 00:06:43.040
from young donors
to patients

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.240
with mild to moderate
Alzheimer's disease.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:49.560
But there may be
an easier way.

00:06:49.560 --> 00:06:52.160
Yes, the injection of blood
from young mice into old

00:06:52.160 --> 00:06:53.840
can improve memory
and learning,

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:57.640
suggesting there's some kind
of restorative youth factor,

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.440
but the injection of blood
from old mice into young

00:07:00.440 --> 00:07:02.280
can worsen memory and learning,

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:03.600
suggesting that instead

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.280
there's some sort
of debilitating aging factor.

00:07:07.280 --> 00:07:09.720
Or maybe the old blood
is just diluting

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:12.080
the revitalizing factor
in the young mouse,

00:07:12.080 --> 00:07:13.720
or, for that matter,
maybe the young blood

00:07:13.720 --> 00:07:16.040
is diluting
the debilitating factor

00:07:16.040 --> 00:07:17.080
in the old mouse.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:19.760
The fact that old blood
appears to make things worse

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:22.320
more than young blood
makes them better

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.400
suggests maybe the latter
is the case,

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.560
but you don't know,
until you put it to the test.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:34.680
Ready for a mind-blowing shift
in perspective?

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.920
Instead of infusing young blood
into old mice,

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.600
researchers at UC Berkeley

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:41.240
just diluted
the blood of old mice

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:44.480
by siphoning off the plasma,
the liquid portion of the blood,

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.360
and essentially replacing
half of it with water.

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.760
If the regeneration seen
in heterochronic parabiosis

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:52.240
and blood transfusions

00:07:52.240 --> 00:07:54.720
were due to some
fountain of youth factor

00:07:54.720 --> 00:07:55.720
in young blood,

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:58.160
then nothing should happen,
right?

00:07:58.160 --> 00:07:59.720
But if all the young blood
was doing

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:02.400
was diluting
some debilitating aging factor,

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.640
then the water should work
just as well,

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:06.480
and it did.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.160
The rejuvenation in the liver

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:10.560
was similar to parabiosis
or transfusion,

00:08:10.560 --> 00:08:14.760
and in the muscles and the brain
it was even better.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.960
So, most if not all the benefits

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:20.720
could be replicated
by simple dilution,

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:24.400
including an improvement
in cognitive capacity.

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:25.400
That's good news,

00:08:25.400 --> 00:08:28.080
since there's already
an FDA approved procedure

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:32.360
in use today known
as therapeutic plasma exchange.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:36.680
It's usually used to filter out
toxins or autoimmune antibodies,

00:08:36.680 --> 00:08:40.280
but why not try using it
to try to dilute old blood

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:42.480
to treat Alzheimer's disease?

00:08:42.480 --> 00:08:46.240
And that's exactly what
researchers did.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:47.640
Hundreds of Alzheimer's patients

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.760
were randomized to a therapeutic
plasma exchange procedure,

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.920
or a placebo (sham) procedure,

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:55.840
and though it didn't seem
to help those

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:58.440
with mild Alzheimer's disease,

00:08:58.440 --> 00:09:02.600
those with moderate Alzheimer's
randomized to the real procedure

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:06.160
experienced about 60% less
cognitive and functional decline

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:08.440
over a period of 14 months.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:11.800
This is in stark contrast
to mainstay treatments

00:09:11.800 --> 00:09:14.040
for Alzheimer's
like the drug memantine

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:15.560
that can help with symptoms

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.720
but don't actually change
or alter

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:19.920
the course of the disease.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.640
And the added advantage
over blood transfusions,

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.040
as a Director of an Institute
of Biomedical Ethics put it:

00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:28.040
"There is something peculiar

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:31.889
about the old literally
feeding on the young."

